      "description": "Watch AS Roma vs Fiorentina highlights and goals from Serie A on ReFooty: Football Highlights Hub. Match played on August 24, 2026. The Serie A season brought a high-stakes encounter to the Stadio Olimpico on August 24, 2026, as AS Roma hosted Fiorentina . Both sides entered the pitch with plenty to prove, setting the stage for a tactical battle characterized by intense midfield duels and moments of individual brilliance. Malen Ignites the Olimpico After a cagey opening period filled with goal kicks and tactical positioning, the deadlock was finally broken in the 27th minute. Bryan Cristante initiated a sharp attacking move, finding Paulo Dybala , who turned provider for Donyell Malen . The forward made no mistake with his left foot, slotting the ball home to give the hosts the lead. Fiorentina struggled to find their rhythm, with Nicolo Fagioli receiving a yellow card shortly after for a dangerous challenge as the visitors tried to stem the Roman tide. A Clinical Second Half Display The second half turned into a masterclass led by the duo of Paulo Dybala and Donyell Malen . In the 52nd minute, Malen struck again with his left foot following another precise assist from Dybala . The momentum stayed firmly with the Giallorossi, and by the 60th minute, the hattrick was complete. A defensive error by Radu Dragusin allowed Dybala to tee up Malen for an easy right-footed finish. The Dutch striker left the field to a standing ovation shortly after, having secured a three-goal cushion for his side. Closing the Show with Precision As the clock ticked down, AS Roma continued to dominate possession, maintaining over 60% of the ball. Fiorentina made several tactical changes, introducing Moise Kean and Rolando Mandragora , but they could not breach the home defense. In the 86th minute, another defensive lapse from Radu Dragusin handed an opportunity to substitute Niccolo Pisilli , who clinically finished with his right foot. The match concluded with a convincing victory for the home side, marking a statement performance in their early-season campaign."
